%changelog
* Fri Jul 24 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-108
- monolithic: work around an upstream gap where the engine never installs
  libmeshoptimizer.a into the SDK and ships an empty IMPORTED INTERFACE
  Findmeshoptimizer.cmake (unlike miniaudio/ogg). Without this, a monolithic
  release game export fails at the launcher link with "undefined symbol:
  meshopt_decodeIndexBuffer / meshopt_decodeVertexBuffer" (libAtom_RPI.Public.a
  references them but no meshoptimizer lib is shipped; the profile build is
  unaffected because meshopt is folded into libAtom_RPI.Public.so with hidden
  visibility). The monolithic %install now installs the static lib the build
  produced and replaces the stub Findmeshoptimizer.cmake with a STATIC IMPORTED
  one, mirroring Findogg.cmake. Validated: `export-project -cfg release` on a
  test project links a 182 MB monolithic GameLauncher with 0 undefined meshopt
  symbols, root-free. Gated to the monolithic bcond; retire when upstream lands.

* Fri Jul 24 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-107
- Add the `monolithic` bcond (off by default). When enabled it does a second
  build+install pass (LY_MONOLITHIC_GAME=ON, release config) that ships the
  Monolithic (release, static) engine permutation alongside the default
  profile (shared) build: lib/Linux/release/Monolithic/*.a plus
  cmake/Platform/Linux/Monolithic/ConfigurationTypes_release.cmake. This is
  what a release game export (Project Manager "Export Launcher", or
  export-project --config release) needs; without it the export fails with
  "No monolithic artifacts are detected in the engine installation." Costs
  ~10 min extra build and ~0.7 GB installed. Exercised on the o3de-experimental
  chroots (realigned to development + monolithic); default channels unchanged.

* Wed Jul 22 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-106
- Force git-clone mode for 3rdParty FetchContent (-DO3DE_FETCHCONTENT_FORCE_GIT=ON).
  Upstream o3de/o3de#19622 (merged 2026-07-16) switched ~10 3rdParty deps
  (googletest, assimp, meshoptimizer, Ogg, vorbis, miniaudio, v-hacd,
  RecastNavigation, Manifold, OpenMesh) from git-clone to URL-archive
  FetchContent. The archives are cached named-by-hash with no extension,
  which CMake older than 4.0 cannot extract. CentOS Stream 10 ships CMake
  3.31.8 and failed at cmake configure on the googletest extract (development
  build 10742547); Fedora 44 and rawhide (CMake 4.x) were unaffected because
  they detect the archive type by content. This forces upstream's own git
  fallback for every dep, restoring the behavior that built green on all
  chroots before 2026-07-16. Retire when #19622's URL path works on CMake
  3.x, or when CS10 ships CMake 4.x.

* Tue Jul 07 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-105
- Snapshot Version delimiter is now channel-aware. Stabilization and
  experimental (pre-release of the current tag) keep the '~' pre-release
  form, but development / qt6 / arbitrary-ref snapshots (post-release,
  tracking o3de/development toward the next release) now use the '^'
  post-release delimiter. The unconditional '~' from the 2026-05-28
  stabilization fix made every development build sort below the bare tag,
  so a single legacy '^' build (20260521) outranked every newer '~' build
  in o3de-development and pinned dev-channel users to a stale snapshot
  they could never upgrade past. With '^', development snapshots sort
  above 2605.0-1 and order monotonically by date.

* Thu Jun 25 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-104
- qt6 PySide6 rpath cleanup: also normalize relative '.'-based RUNPATH
  entries, not just the absolute CI path. The pyside6-6.10.2-rev3
  shiboken6 executable ships '$ORIGIN:./../lib'; the './../lib' relative
  entry trips check-rpaths ERROR 0004 and failed %%install on the first
  development-tip build after the qt6 merge into development. The cleanup
  now converts a leading './' (or a lone '.') to $ORIGIN before keeping
  only $ORIGIN entries, so shiboken6 resolves to '$ORIGIN:$ORIGIN/../lib'.
  The absolute CI-path case is unchanged. qt6-gated; Qt5 channels
  evaluate the exact prior behavior.

* Fri Jun 05 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-103
- qt6 builds: extend the qt6-gated __requires_exclude to all four
  dangling auto-Requires found by a full 192-capability audit of the
  RPM against the F44 repos (the -99 fix covered only the first
  reported one). New exclusions: libjpeg.so.8 + libtiff.so.5 (the qt
  package's imageformat plugins link the Ubuntu build host's sonames;
  Qt skips unloadable plugins gracefully, jpeg/tiff QImage formats
  unavailable until upstream rebuilds them) and libclang-1[0-9] (the
  dev-era DXC links host libclang-15 and no longer bundles it; the
  binary is runtime-broken on Fedora regardless, system_dxc overlay is
  the real fix). Qt5 channels evaluate the exact prior regex.

* Thu Jun 04 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-102
- Add Patch0016 (TIFF_DISABLE_DEPRECATED before <tiffio.h> in the three
  consumer TUs) and reactivate the system_tiff Stage 1 swap on the
  experimental channel, parked since -17. Modern libtiff guards its
  legacy non-prefixed typedefs behind TIFF_DISABLE_DEPRECATED; with all
  consumers already on the C99 names (Patch0007 / o3de/o3de#19734) the
  guard removes the tiff side of the int64/uint64 collision with
  CryCommon/BaseTypes.h without touching CryCommon, sidestepping the
  Option A failure mode from -27. Gated on system_tiff; shipping
  channels unchanged. If experimental validates, this is the upstream
  pitch and the Bundling Library Exception (Option C) retires.

* Thu Jun 04 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-101
- launcher: ROS2 environment autodetect. Detect /opt/ros/<distro>/ and
  import the ROS env (AMENT_*/COLCON_*/ROS_*/RMW_*, LD_LIBRARY_PATH,
  PATH, CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH) so menu-launched sessions can load the ROS2
  gem's librcl/librmw without a manually sourced shell. Newest distro
  wins (codenames are alphabetical by release); O3DE_ROS_DISTRO pins
  one; O3DE_DISABLE_ROS2=1 opts out. PYTHONPATH is deliberately NOT
  imported (system-Python site-packages poison the bundled 3.10 venv).

* Thu Jun 04 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-100
- launcher: skip the Wayland QT_FONT_DPI=100 workaround on qt6-era
  builds (runtime-detected via libQt6Core.so.6 in BIN_DIR). The fixed
  DPI papers over a Qt 5.15 auto-DPI bug that does not exist on Qt 6,
  where it would instead suppress correct per-monitor scaling. Qt5
  channels keep the workaround unchanged.

* Thu Jun 04 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-99
- qt6 builds: drop the dangling libQt6Qml.so.6 auto-Require via a
  qt6-gated __requires_exclude extension. The deployed lupdate
  (LinguistTools) links libQt6Qml but the qt 3p package deploys no Qml
  libraries, leaving an unsatisfiable Require (incl. Qt_6_PRIVATE_API)
  that made the o3de-development-qt6 RPMs dnf-uninstallable. lupdate is
  the only payload binary linking Qml (full payload NEEDED scan,
  2026-06-04) and the PySide6 QtQml module is not shipped. Qt5 channels
  unchanged (the exclusion is inside the qt6 bcond).

* Wed Jun 03 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-98
- Add Patch0014 + the swap_hook bcond: a prototype of the central
  system-swap download hook proposed upstream (one LY_USE_SYSTEM_<NAME>
  guard inside ly_download_associated_package, replacing Patch0006's
  per-line BuiltInPackages gating; the trailing find_package at each
  call site then resolves our Find<X> shims). Default OFF, so every
  shipping channel builds exactly as before; the o3de-experimental
  chroots set it to validate the mechanism for the upstream proposal.
  Patch0009 is kept under the hook (gem-local gate, coexists). Patch0013
  is swapped for Patch0015 under the hook: the same two runtime hunks
  (PAL_linux dependency removal, VK_LAYER_PATH SetEnv flip) minus the
  BuiltInPackages associate-gate hunk, whose context assumed Patch0006
  and which the lazy model makes unnecessary (associate lines only
  register; with the reference removed, nothing ever resolves it).
- Pass -DLY_USE_SYSTEM_IMATH=ON alongside -DLY_USE_SYSTEM_OPENEXR=ON.
  The OpenEXR 3rdParty association declares TARGETS OpenEXR Imath, and
  the hook keys per target, so without the Imath flag a 3rdParty::Imath
  reference would still download the bundled OpenEXR package. Inert for
  the Patch0006 path (nothing there reads the Imath flag).

* Tue Jun 02 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-97
- Add a qt6 bcond to fence the Qt6-migration-specific build deps and steps
  off from the Qt5 channels. The o3de/o3de:qt6 branch (vanilla Qt6 +
  PySide6) is a 26.10-track forward test only (it is NOT backported to
  26.05); the o3de-development-qt6 COPR channel sets --rpmbuild-with qt6.
- Move the dbus-devel BuildRequires (bundled Qt6 QtDBus link dep) under the
  qt6 bcond, so the Qt5 stable/testing/development channels no longer pull
  it. Behavior-neutral for them (it was an unused BR there at -96).
- Under the qt6 bcond, add an %%install rpath cleanup (via patchelf) for the
  bundled PySide6 binaries. The pyside6 3rdParty package bakes an absolute
  o3de CI build path (/home/runner/...) and a misplaced $ORIGIN into the
  RUNPATH of shiboken6 / libpyside6 / the *.abi3.so modules, which Fedora's
  check-rpaths QA rejects (ERROR 0002 + 0008). The cleanup strips the dead
  CI path and keeps only the $ORIGIN-relative entries. Self-limited to
  files that carry the CI path. Surfaced by qt6 build 10549660.

* Tue Jun 02 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-96
- Gate the Lua 5.5 compat patches (Patch0010 lua_newstate, Patch0011
  WatchesPanel LUA_NUMTAGS) on the system_lua bcond. Lua 5.5 is only ever
  reached through the system swap (the bundled package is Lua-5.4.4, and
  F44's system Lua is 5.4.8; only rawhide's system Lua is 5.5), so for
  every all-bundled build these shims are inert and need not apply. Beyond
  the correctness tidy-up this stops them failing %%prep when an all-bundled
  upstream branch reworks one of the patched files, which is exactly what
  the Qt6 branch did to WatchesPanel.cpp. Behavior-neutral for the
  system_lua channels (testing, stable-with-swap), which still apply both.
- BuildRequires: dbus-devel. The bundled Qt6's libQt6DBus.so.6 carries
  versioned references to libdbus-1 and must resolve them at link time;
  Fedora's minimal mock buildroot does not pull libdbus in by default, so
  a Qt6 build fails at link. Harmless for the current Qt5 bundle (its
  QtDBus is not linked this way). Enables the in-progress Qt6 migration.

* Sat May 30 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-95
- Recommends: xdg-utils. The Project Manager's 'Open Project folder'
  action and the "open build log" / "open export output" links use
  Qt's QDesktopServices::openUrl, which on Linux shells out to xdg-open
  (from xdg-utils). Without it those actions silently no-op on a minimal
  install. Completes the Project Manager helper-tool dependency coverage
  alongside the -92 xcb-util, -93 tk8/tcl8/tix, and -94 cmake-gui/ninja
  additions.
* Fri May 29 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-94
- Pull in the Project Manager's build + CMake GUI tooling.
  - Recommends: ninja-build. The PM 'Build' action and `cmake --build`
    use the Ninja generator; ninja was a BuildRequires for the engine
    build but was missing from the runtime project-build deps, so a
    project 'Build' failed on a clean install. Upstream's .deb declares
    it for the same reason.
  - Recommends: cmake-gui. The PM 'Open CMake GUI' action execs the
    system cmake-gui binary, which on Fedora is a SEPARATE package from
    cmake, so a clean install with cmake still failed 'Open CMake GUI'
    with "Failed to start CMake GUI". Community-verified 2026-05-29.
  Cross-platform note: upstream's .deb/Snap declare neither cmake-gui
  nor a Tk runtime, so the tkinter and CMake-GUI Project Manager tools
  are broken on a clean Debian/Ubuntu install too; see upstream-drafts/.
* Fri May 29 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-93
- Make the editor's tkinter GUI helper dialogs work on Fedora 44.
  Open Export Settings and Open Android Project Generator drive the
  editor's bundled Python via tkinter (Open CMake GUI is separate; it
  execs the system cmake-gui binary, addressed in -94). That bundled
  Python's _tkinter is built against Tk 8.6 (DT_NEEDED libtk8.6.so +
  libtcl8.6.so). Fedora 44 moved the default tk/tcl to 9.x and split
  the 8.6 runtime into the tk8 / tcl8 packages, so a clean install lacks
  libtk8.6.so and these tools failed with
  "ImportError: libtk8.6.so: cannot open shared object file".
  - Recommends: tk8, tcl8 (the Tk/Tcl 8.6 runtime _tkinter needs).
  - Recommends: tix (Export Settings uses tkinter.tix).
  - Launcher exports TCLLIBPATH=/usr/lib64/tcl so the bundled Tcl finds
    the system Tix package (Fedora installs it under /usr/lib64/tcl/,
    which the bundled Tcl's auto_path otherwise does not cover, so tix
    alone did not resolve Export Settings).
  The bundled Python is fetched per-user post-install, so rpm cannot
  auto-detect these from its _tkinter.so; they are listed explicitly.
  Community report: adwglds on Fedora 44. Both failures reproduced and
  verified fixed locally against the bundled python 3.10.
* Fri May 29 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-92
- Add the xcb-util-*-devel suite to the project-build Recommends:
  xcb-util-devel, xcb-util-image-devel, xcb-util-keysyms-devel,
  xcb-util-renderutil-devel, xcb-util-wm-devel.
  Building a project against the installed engine compiles against
  Qt's xcb platform plugin headers, which need the XCB util suite.
  These were already BuildRequires for the engine build itself but
  were missing from the runtime project-build Recommends, so a clean
  environment without them (e.g. a Fedora Toolbox) failed a project
  build until they were installed by hand.
  Field report: alex7900 on Bazzite 43 / Fedora 44 Toolbox hit the
  missing xcb-util-keysyms-devel; the rest of the suite is added
  alongside it to match the BuildRequires set. Recommends (weak) so
  runtime-only installs can still opt out with install_weak_deps=False.
* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-91
- Escape literal section markers in changelog entries.
  CS10's RPM 4.19 parser misreads any unescaped %%<section> in
  %%include'd content as a section start; the first such token after
  the legitimate %%changelog header looks like a second %%install /
  %%build section, breaking SRPM build.
  Caught by o3de-testing first-fire build 10522782: CS10 chroot
  failed at buildsrpm with "error: line 809: second %%install"
  referring to a prose mention of an `%%install-time symlink` in the
  -82 RapidXML walkback entry which CS10's parser interpreted as a
  section header. F44 and rawhide RPM 6.x parse the same content
  idempotently; only CS10 trips.
  Memory note: project_cs10_debuginfo_quirk.md (the second of two
  CS10 RPM 4.19 quirks documented there).
  Six instances escaped across the changelog history. The first-line
  %%changelog header that the spec's %%include expects stays
  unescaped.
  Same SRPM content as -90 except for the escape characters; no
  functional change to the built package.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-90
- Introduce hellaenergy/o3de-testing channel (pre-promotion soak for
  stable, Fedora updates-testing semantics). Promotion flow:
  main HEAD to testing (~48h soak) to stable. Adds COPR_PROJECT_TESTING
  Makefile variable + copr-testing / copr-testing-and-test targets,
  README + CONTRIBUTING + ARCHITECTURE (with Mermaid diagram update) +
  FEDORA_ROADMAP + POST_RELEASE documentation sweep, CI workflow cron-
  poll default changed to o3de-testing, SBOM externalReferences updated
  with new "distribution" entries for both stable and testing COPR
  channels. Out-of-repo: COPR project hellaenergy/o3de-testing created
  with stable's swap set mirrored (17-item with_opts; NO experimental
  swap activations) and additional_repos pointing at o3de-dependencies.
  Per the Release-bump-for-SRPM-coherence rule: -90 ships the new SBOM
  + spec content; no functional change to the binary RPM.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-89
- Auto-apply QT_FONT_DPI=100 workaround for Wayland XWayland.
  Community report 2026-05-28 (adwaita on Fedora 44 + GNOME Wayland +
  4K + amdgpu): bundled Qt 5.15-rev9 mis-detects font scale on
  Wayland XWayland; without `QT_FONT_DPI=100` fonts in Project
  Manager render at the wrong size. Same workaround used by the
  upstream Debian .deb package (so it's a Qt-bundle issue, not a
  Fedora-specific packaging issue).
  Fix: the launcher script now sets QT_FONT_DPI=100 automatically
  when XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland AND the user hasn't already set
  QT_FONT_DPI. X11 sessions are unaffected. Users who want a
  different value can still export QT_FONT_DPI before launch.
  Also: README Known Limitations section gains two entries:
  - Bundled Qt 5.15-rev9 ships only xcb / offscreen / minimal
    platform plugins; no wayland plugin. System qt5-qtwayland
    cannot substitute because the bundle is isolated. GNOME / KDE
    Wayland users get XCB via XWayland. Will resolve with the Qt 6
    migration targeted for 26.10.0.
  - Qt 5.15-rev9 auto-DPI mis-detection on Wayland XWayland;
    automatically worked around by the launcher as of -89.
  This is a downstream-only doc + launcher change; no engine source
  touched, no upstream PR needed.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-88
- Auto-pull o3de2605-devel via Recommends from the main package.
  Community report 2026-05-28 (Adwaita installing 2605.0-1 from
  hellaenergy/o3de): cmake configure succeeded, `cmake --build` failed
  at ninja with `libAzGameFramework.a, needed by libDefaultProject.so,
  missing and no known rule to make it`. Root cause: the static archives
  needed for ANY project build (DefaultProject's GameLauncher target was
  the trigger; same applies to MultiplayerSample, NewspaperDeliveryGame,
  and any user-created project from Project Manager) live in
  o3de2605-devel, not the main runtime package. The user had only
  installed o3de2605 because the README framed -devel as "for native
  gem development" -- which mis-scoped the actual requirement.
  Fix: add `Recommends: %{name}-devel = %{version}-%{release}` to the
  main package's Recommends list. dnf pulls in both by default; users
  who want runtime-only (rare; headless server hosts running pre-built
  binaries) can still opt out via `--setopt=install_weak_deps=False`.
  Recommends rather than Requires preserves the headless-runtime-only
  use case as a legitimate path.
  Also: README clarity pass. The Subpackages overview table now
  describes -devel as the link-time material every project build
  needs (not "for native gem development"); the install snippet shows
  `dnf install o3de2605` pulling -devel automatically; the consumer
  example line drops the separate -devel install (auto-recommended).
  Doesn't help users already on -1 (they need a manual
  `sudo dnf install o3de2605-devel`); fix lands on the next stable
  rebuild.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-87
- Walk back system_rapidxml: Fedora's stock rapidxml-devel lacks the
  Amazon Lumberyard API extensions the engine depends on.
  Experimental build 10522249 (-86) failed at %%build:
    Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Serialization/ObjectStream.cpp:2069:
      error: no member named 'isError' in 'rapidxml::xml_document<>'
    Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Serialization/ObjectStream.cpp:2071:
      error: no member named 'getError' in 'rapidxml::xml_document<>'
  Root cause: the bundled `RapidXML-1.13-rev1-multiplatform` package
  contains Amazon Lumberyard's "AZ Extention" patches that add
  `m_isError` / `m_error[512]` state plus `isError()` / `getError()` /
  `resetError()` methods on `xml_document`. These replace upstream
  RapidXML's exception-throwing error model with return-value error
  reporting, which is required because O3DE builds with
  `-D_HAS_EXCEPTIONS=0` (no C++ exceptions). Fedora's `rapidxml-devel`
  ships the upstream form without these extensions; the engine code
  cannot compile against it.
  Same shape as libtiff: a "-rev1" / "_az" suffixed bundle is a
  patched fork, not a re-versioned upstream. The Find shim, the
  bcond `%bcond_with system_rapidxml` (defaulted off), the Source46
  declaration, the BR/Recommends/cp/-D blocks, and the Patch0006
  gate all remain in place as activation framework, in case a future
  rebuild of the patched fork ships via COPR. For now,
  `--with system_rapidxml` drops from SRPM_EXPERIMENTAL_FLAGS and the
  spec-parse-experimental define list, and the matching chroot
  with_opts entries will be removed via copr-cli edit-chroot before
  the next experimental build fires.
  Other three swaps from today (system_rapidjson, system_xxhash,
  system_cityhash) verified upstream-only via grep for "AZ Extention"
  / O3DE-specific patches in the bundled headers; no extension calls
  in the engine's consumer files. Those three stay active.
  Memory note added: `feedback_check_for_bundled_patched_apis.md`.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-86
- Fix Patch0006: regenerate against stabilization/26050 snapshot pin.
  Experimental build 10522201 failed at %%prep on all three chroots
  (F44, rawhide, CS10) with "1 out of 1 hunk FAILED" applying
  0006-builtinpackages-gate-mikkelsen-on-system.patch. Root cause:
  the patch was regenerated against my local dev-tip checkout
  (~/PROJECTS/o3de) earlier today in the xxhash and cityhash commits,
  but dev-tip has dropped the `assimp-5.4.3-rev3-linux` line from
  BuiltInPackages_linux_x86_64.cmake while stabilization/26050 still
  carries it. The dev-tip-derived patch's hunk shape did not match
  the snapshot's actual layout.
  Fix: regenerate Patch0006 against the snapshot pin commit 8e75050
  via `git show 8e750500:cmake/.../BuiltInPackages_linux_x86_64.cmake`,
  then verify clean with `patch -p1 --dry-run` against the same
  source. Captured the lesson as a new third paragraph in the patch's
  explanation header so future regenerations use the right basis.
  This makes -86 the first revision that should build successfully
  on experimental with the full Stage 1 swap set including the new
  four (rapidxml, rapidjson, xxhash, cityhash).

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-85
- Stage 1 swap #18: system_cityhash against the license-clean COPR
  rebuild o3de2605-cityhash.
  Fourth multiplatform-section gate (after rapidxml, rapidjson, xxhash)
  and the first one that routes through a COPR-rebuilt dep instead of
  a Fedora package. cityhash is not in Fedora, so we built a clean
  SRPM from upstream google/cityhash at commit f5dc541 (2022-07-19)
  and pushed it to hellaenergy/o3de-dependencies as o3de2605-cityhash.
  Package layout follows the o3deNNNN-<dep> versioning convention
  (same as o3de2605-spirv-cross, o3de2605-dxc-spirv, o3de2605-mcpp-az).
  Ships:
    - /usr/lib64/libcityhash.so + libcityhash.so.0 + libcityhash.so.0.x
      (main package: shared library for engine link)
    - /usr/lib64/libcityhash.a + /usr/include/city.h
      (devel subpackage: headers + static archive)
  Engine consumes via #include <city.h> in
  Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Utils/TypeHash.cpp; no subdir bridge
  needed since /usr/include is naturally on the include path. AzCore
  cmake target 3rdParty::cityhash links the shared library (the
  bundled package shipped pre-compiled static .a; we ship .so instead).
  Spec wires BR/Requires/Recommends to o3de2605-cityhash-devel /
  o3de2605-cityhash. cityhash's citycrc.h (SSE 4.2 CRC-accelerated
  variant) is NOT in the devel subpackage because no O3DE consumer
  uses it and gating on --enable-sse4.2 would narrow the build matrix.
  COPR build for o3de2605-cityhash: build 10522180 in
  hellaenergy/o3de-dependencies (pushed 2026-05-28). Engine swap
  cannot fire until that build succeeds + the chroots are configured
  to pull o3de-dependencies. No engine COPR build fires from this
  commit; next experimental run picks it up.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-84
- Stage 1 swap #17: system_xxhash against Fedora's xxhash-devel.
  Third multiplatform-section gate (after rapidxml and rapidjson).
  Engine consumes xxhash via #include <xxhash/xxhash.h>; Fedora ships
  flat /usr/include/xxhash.h, so the find shim emits a subdir-bridge
  wrapper in the build dir.
  Header-only (no runtime libxxhash.so dependency). The bundled
  xxhash package on packages.o3de.org is also header-only -- its
  Findxxhash.cmake creates an INTERFACE IMPORTED target with just
  an include dir, no library link. The engine uses xxhash via its
  static-inline functions (XXH64, XXH3_64bits, XXH3_128bits in
  AssetProcessor + AssetBuilderSDK). The system swap mirrors that:
  include path only, no link to libxxhash.so.
  Version note: engine pins 0.7.4; Fedora ships 0.8.3. xxhash has
  stable C ABI from 0.7 forward; the 0.7 to 0.8 transition added new
  functions and improved performance without breaking the existing
  API. Engine consumers continue to work.
  Patch0006 hunks regenerated via diff -u against a fresh source
  copy to avoid hand-maintained hunk-header drift. The old
  hand-written two-hunk form had accumulating dst_start shift errors
  as each swap was added; diff-generated single-hunk form is
  authoritative going forward.
  No COPR build fires; next experimental run picks it up.

* Thu May 28 2026 Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com> - 2605.0-83
- Stage 1 swap #16: system_rapidjson against Fedora's rapidjson-devel.
  Simplest possible Stage 1 swap yet. Fedora's rapidjson-devel ships
  the same `<rapidjson/<name>.h>` subdir layout the engine consumes,
  so no subdir-bridge wrappers are needed (unlike RapidXML). The
  find shim just locates the rapidjson/ subdir under /usr/include
  and adds the parent to the include path.
  Version note: engine pins 1.1.0; Fedora ships post-1.1.0 main-branch
  snapshot 1.1.0^20241222git24b5e7a. RapidJSON has not removed
  published API in the interval; engine consumers continue to work
  against the newer headers.
  Build-time-only (header-only library; no runtime .so dependency).
  No COPR build fires; will pick up on the next experimental build
  invocation. Stabilization stays at the 14-pack.

